# bridge.conf.example — every value the Slack bridge installer reads.
#
#   cp bridge.conf.example bridge.conf && chmod 600 bridge.conf
#   $EDITOR bridge.conf                                      # nine values, none secret
#   python3 scripts/pipeline_bridge_setup.py compose         # read what it builds
#
# Then docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, step by step. The installer reports EVERY bad
# value in one pass.
#
# FORMAT: `KEY=value`, one per line. `#` starts a comment only at the start of a
# line. Parsed, never sourced. A key set twice is an error naming both lines.
# NO KEY HERE EVER HOLDS A CREDENTIAL: a value shaped like one is refused, by
# key name only. The bridge app's token goes in at `token`'s hidden prompt.
#
# It runs beside an existing Stage E install: the same role account, its clone
# of this kit, and its env file, which already holds the owner's tracker key.

# --- Required --------------------------------------------------------------

# The dispatcher's role account. The bridge runs as it (owner decision,
# 2026-10-10): its own Slack app keeps it apart from any other app.
ROLE_ACCOUNT=_exampledispatch

# That account's clone of this kit, which the job runs from: the one the Stage E
# daemons exec from. The Stage E installer's `code` step is the only thing that
# moves it, and stops this job while it does. The installer refuses any other
# clone: nothing would ever move it.
ROLE_KIT_CLONE=~/.stage-e/kit

# The private channel(s) the bridge reads and answers in, such as the one the
# notifier pages in. Comma-separated. Invite the bridge app to each (card CK-B1).
CHANNEL_IDS=C0123456789

# The tracker user id of the owner, whose key the bridge acts with.
OWNER_USER_ID=00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001

# The work teams whose tickets the bridge will ask about. Comma-separated.
TEAM_KEYS=PROD

# The LaunchDaemon label, reverse-DNS.
JOB_LABEL=com.example.bridge

# The bridge app's own bot user id: its member ID, copied from the app's profile
# in Slack (card CK-B1). The job refuses any token that answers to another bot.
BOT_USER_ID=U0123456789

# Your own Slack member ID (your profile, then Copy member ID). The tech lead answers in
# your DM with the bridge app, which is on by default: see TECH_LEAD_TURN below. More
# members, comma-separated. Set it once the app has its manifest (card CK-B1). Required
# while TECH_LEAD_TURN=yes, the default: the installer refuses it empty then.
DM_USER_IDS=U0123456789

# The claude program's full path, as `command -v claude` prints it: the tech lead's turn
# runs it. The job runs under launchd, whose PATH is short, so a bare name won't do.
# Required while TECH_LEAD_TURN=yes, the default.
TURN_BIN=/opt/homebrew/bin/claude

# --- Optional, with their defaults -----------------------------------------

# Whether a verified yes acts. no: the bridge asks and records only. yes: a
# verified yes to `plan <ticket id>` moves that idea into Plan it, and a yes to
# `approve <epic id>` moves the epic to READY_STATE and hands its children to
# AGENT_USER_NAME, in dependency order. Both act as the owner.
# Start with no, watch it ask, then set yes and run `install --apply`.
#ACT=no
#PLAN_IT_STATE=Plan it

# The dispatcher's app user, by its display name in the tracker: who an
# approved epic's children are handed to. Empty refuses every `approve`.
#AGENT_USER_NAME=
# How many pieces may be started and unfinished at once, counted across every approved plan
# on one team (KIT-417), not per plan.
#EPIC_WIP=1
# The queue's config file, as the role account sees it (KIT-454). With it, the bridge asks the
# queue what may start, inside the working window, and starts it. Empty: the bridge's own
# hand-over of approved plans. How often it asks, in seconds.
#QUEUE_CONFIG=
#QUEUE_EVERY_SECONDS=900
# The to-do state an approved epic moves to.
#READY_STATE=Todo
# The done state an approved epic moves to once every piece of it is merged.
# The bridge then posts a wrap-up, and asks before it files the review notes
# left as one new ticket in the team.
#DONE_STATE=Done
# The state a child sits in while its pull request waits for your merge. It is
# the lane a ticket moves to when review is done, and setup-board names it
# Needs Approval. Spell it as the tracker shows it, comma-separated if your teams
# name it differently. The bridge says a child there is waiting for you, and never
# tells you to hand it over again.
#HANDOFF_STATES=Needs Approval

# Answering a stuck session (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, Step 10). The notifier's
# STATE_DIR, exactly as notifier.conf sets it: the bridge reads the pings the
# notifier records there. And the notifier app's own bot user id (its member ID,
# from its profile in Slack): with it, a reply under a ping the notifier did not
# record is refused aloud instead of ignored, and `verify` needs the pings file.
#NOTIFIER_STATE_DIR=~/.stage-e/state
#NOTIFIER_BOT_USER_ID=

# Watching the notifier (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, Step 11). The notifier's
# JOB_LABEL, exactly as notifier.conf sets it. Each pass then reads how often
# launchd runs the notifier, from its plist, and its heartbeat in
# NOTIFIER_STATE_DIR. When the notifier is quiet for three of its intervals plus
# two minutes, keeps failing, or can't be read, the bridge says so once in the
# first channel, and once more when it is back. Keep INTERVAL_SECONDS at most
# half the notifier's, or `verify` says BLOCKED. launchd keeps the interval the
# job was loaded with, so after changing INTERVAL_SECONDS, reload the job: card
# CK-B3, then card CK-B2. Empty: the watch is off. Leave it empty only on a
# machine with no notifier.
#NOTIFIER_JOB_LABEL=

# The only Slack user ids who may answer a question. Empty means any full
# member of the workspace: guests, bots and other workspaces are always refused.
#ALLOWED_SLACK_USERS=

# Working in your DM (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, "Working in your DM"): DM_USER_IDS, set
# above, names whose DM with the bridge app it also reads and answers in, with messages
# of their own, so each one notifies. It may be empty only with TECH_LEAD_TURN=no: then
# no DM is read.
# A question in your DM you haven't answered gets one reminder this many hours
# after it was asked, 0 to 23. 0: none. A question expires after 24 hours, and
# says so where it was asked.
#QUESTION_REMINDER_HOURS=4

# The tech lead (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, "The tech lead"). yes, the default: what
# you write in your DM that is not a command, a yes or a no is answered there by one
# turn of the claude CLI, TURN_BIN above, over the tech lead's tools. It needs
# DM_USER_IDS, TURN_BIN, and the token `writer-token` stores. no: that text gets one
# line on what the bridge can do there.
#TECH_LEAD_TURN=yes
# The model that answers.
#TURN_MODEL=sonnet
# The NAME the token is under. `writer-token` puts it there, from `claude setup-token`.
# A name of its own, never another key's.
#TURN_TOKEN_ENV=CLAUDE_WRITER_OAUTH_TOKEN
# The tool server's config, and the file it reads the tracker key from. `install
# --apply` writes both as the role account, the key from the env file, mode 0600.
#TURN_TOOLS_CONFIG=~/.stage-e/tech-lead/tools.json
#TURN_KEY_FILE=~/.stage-e/tech-lead/tracker-key
# The folder the tool server's acting tools record what they want asked in.
# `install --apply` makes it, mode 0700. The bridge empties it after each turn.
#TURN_OUTBOX_DIR=~/.stage-e/tech-lead/outbox

# The cutover (KIT-492) took these keys out: ASK_TECH_LEAD, CHAT_BOT_USER_ID,
# BACK_ROOM_CHANNEL_ID, FORWARD_HISTORY_MESSAGES, FORWARD_HISTORY_CHARS, WRITER,
# WRITER_BIN, WRITER_MODEL, WRITER_TOKEN_ENV, TECH_LEAD_WAKE, WAKE_MAX_PER_DAY,
# WAKE_QUIET_HOURS and WAKE_PR_STUCK_HOURS. A line with one still loads and does
# nothing; `verify`'s old-paths row names it, so you can take it out.

# The daily stand-up (docs/STANDUP.md). on: a morning rundown and an afternoon
# wrap go to the DM of the first of DM_USER_IDS, written from the status file,
# and a piece that starts gets no message of its own: the wrap lists it.
# Needs DM_USER_IDS. Times are local and 24-hour; the afternoon must be more
# than 3 hours after the morning. Days are comma-separated, mon to sun. Turn it
# on or off with the installer's `standup` command, or with `install --apply`.
#STANDUP=off
#STANDUP_MORNING_AT=09:00
#STANDUP_AFTERNOON_AT=15:00
#STANDUP_DAYS=mon, tue, wed, thu, fri, sat
# The status job's file: stage-e.conf's STATUS_FILE, if you set one.
#STANDUP_STATUS_FILE=~/.stage-e/status/pipeline-status.json
# Optional, and normally left empty (KIT-718): the stand-up covers everything
# the pipeline runs, every repository and plan the status file lists. Name
# repositories here, OWNER/REPO, comma-separated, only to narrow it to theirs.
#STANDUP_REPOS=
# The afternoon wrap's spend (KIT-471). The day plan file adds one line: today's
# spend against the day's planned load and hard stop. The usage file adds a short
# week on the weekly day (one of STANDUP_DAYS; empty: the last of them). Empty
# files: off, and the wrap is what it was.
#STANDUP_DAY_PLAN_FILE=~/.stage-e/queue/day-plan.json
#STANDUP_USAGE_FILE=~/.stage-e/usage/usage.json
#STANDUP_WEEKLY_DAY=sat
# The sprint (KIT-574): one fixed line in both messages saying which day of the plan's
# weekly sprint it is, when the week resets and how full the window is. The sprint is the
# plan's week, from the usage file, so it moves when the reset moves. Needs
# STANDUP_USAGE_FILE. yes or no.
#STANDUP_SPRINT=no
# Sprint planning (KIT-575): the file the queue's morning job writes each new sprint's proposal into (the same path as
# SPRINT_PLAN_FILE in queue.conf). The stand-up sends a waiting proposal to your DM once; your reply
# `plan sprint <date>` (the date it names) records the plan in the file. Nothing starts from the proposal alone.
# Needs STANDUP_SPRINT=yes. Empty: off.
#STANDUP_SPRINT_PLAN_FILE=
# The health watch's status file (its HEALTH_STATUS_FILE in update.conf; KIT-609). When the machine's memory or the
# budget's burn rate is a concern, both messages add ONE plain line saying what, how far over, and what the system
# is doing about it. When all is usual they say nothing. Empty: off.
#STANDUP_HEALTH_FILE=
# The sprint review (KIT-576): the path of its config file (print an example with
# `python3 scripts/pipeline_sprint_review.py --example-config`). On the sprint's last working day, from the afternoon
# time, the stand-up writes a recap of the week as a Linear document and sends ONE message that links it. Built by
# code from the usage file, GitHub and your weekly budget. Needs STANDUP_SPRINT=yes. Empty: off.
#STANDUP_SPRINT_REVIEW_CONFIG=
# A link in the tech lead's answer stays a link in your DM only when it goes to
# one of these hosts, or under one, judged by where it really goes, never by its
# words. Any other reaches your DM as plain text, its dots and colons in square
# brackets, and a line says so. An answer with something shaped like a password
# or a key never reaches your DM: a line says that instead. Comma-separated.
#TECH_LEAD_LINK_HOSTS=linear.app, github.com

# Your own clear ask acts after a stop window (ACT-4): with DIRECT_ASKS=yes, a
# DM message of yours that names plan or replan and up to DIRECT_MAX_TICKETS
# tickets acts DIRECT_GRACE_SECONDS after the bridge says what it will do, with
# no question, unless you write again first. Needs ACT=yes, DM_USER_IDS and
# AGENT_USER_NAME. Turn it on with `direct-asks --apply`, which asks one yes.
# DIRECT_MAX_TICKETS only sets where asking starts: more tickets get one
# combined question. One first read DIRECT_MAX_AGE_MINUTES after it was sent
# gets a question instead.
#DIRECT_ASKS=no
#DIRECT_GRACE_SECONDS=90
#DIRECT_MAX_TICKETS=3
#DIRECT_MAX_AGE_MINUTES=10

# The dispatcher's own env file. With it set, `token` refuses a token it already
# holds, and `verify`'s old-paths row checks it no longer names the old chat app
# (KIT-492). Unset, that row is UNKNOWN: a chat lane it did not check is never off.
# It must be a different file from ENV_FILE: the dispatcher hands this one to every
# session it starts.
#DISPATCHER_ENV_FILE=

#ENV_FILE=~/.stage-e/env
# The NAME the bridge app's token is stored under. Never SLACK_BOT_TOKEN, the
# dispatcher's chat app's own name.
#SLACK_TOKEN_ENV=BRIDGE_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN
# The NAME of the owner's tracker key, which Stage E already stores.
#LINEAR_KEY_ENV=STAGE_E_LINEAR_API_KEY
#BRIDGE_CONFIG=~/.stage-e/bridge.json
#STATE_DIR=~/.stage-e/state
#INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
# What you call your tech lead: the Slack app's name and its bot's. Left unset, `manifest`
# asks you once and saves your answer here. Change it later to rename (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md).
#APP_NAME=Tech lead
# The project's name (KIT-477). With it set, the Slack app is called "Tech lead", a middle dot,
# then this name, in the manifest `compose` prints and in what `verify` expects Slack to say.
# Up to 23 characters: letters, digits, spaces, dots, dashes and underscores. APP_NAME is used
# when this is empty. A second project has its own conf, its own app and its own token.
#PROJECT_NAME=
# A square PNG file, 512 to 2000 pixels, for the app's picture. The installer only checks it;
# a manifest cannot carry a picture, so card CK-B1 says where to upload it by hand.
#APP_AVATAR=

# Instant replies (KIT-561; card CK-B9). Off by default: the job that runs once a minute answers a
# DM, as it always has. PUSH_MODE=yes adds a second job that holds Slack's push connection
# (Socket Mode) so a DM is answered within seconds; the once-a-minute job stays as the catch-up.
# It needs TECH_LEAD_TURN=yes and its own LaunchDaemon label. The manifest `manifest` prints then
# turns Socket Mode on, subscribes to DM messages, and makes the app an agent app (Slack does not
# let an app go back from that). The app-level token goes in at `push-token`'s hidden prompt,
# under PUSH_TOKEN_ENV, never in this file. `push` does the rest in one go, and sets PUSH_MODE and
# PUSH_JOB_LABEL (JOB_LABEL with -push after it) in this file itself after you say yes.
#PUSH_MODE=no
#PUSH_JOB_LABEL=com.example.bridge-push
#PUSH_TOKEN_ENV=BRIDGE_PUSH_APP_TOKEN
